We explored the hypothesis that newly characterized long-read CAGE transcripts could be translated to provide additional antigens. To identify potential novel proteins, we generated a H1299 custom proteome by combining a LRCAGE proteome with a variant-aware GENCODE proteome (
Fig. 4
A). We prepared the LRCAGE proteome by in silico translation of newly characterized transcripts (Methods). Of 12,664 proteins encoded by these transcripts, 10,774 were unannotated proteins, which had the potential to produce noncanonical antigens (
Fig. 4
B,C). For ORFs, 94% of unannotated proteins joined with GENCODE-ORFs, <1% joined with endogenous viral element (EVE)-ORFs, and the remaining 6% joined neither ORF type (
Fig. 4
D). Among EVE-ORFs, LINE-1 ORF1 and ORF2 contributed the most followed by
pol
,
gag
, and
env
(
Fig. 4
D). Of the 25 proteins joining LINE-1 ORFs, 68% (17) and 48% (12) were encoded by L1- and L1PA2-derived transcripts, respectively (
Supplemental Table S4
).
In H1299 whole-cell lysate LC-MS/MS data (
Choi et al. 2020
